## 1.3 Design Defence

The hierarchy reduces code duplication by placing common details such as name and ID in the base class Staff. Each derived class implements its own pay() function. When a new staff category is added, a new derived class can be created without modifying the existing classes. The use of virtual functions allows all staff members to be processed through a common base-class pointer. This makes the program reusable, maintainable, and easier to extend.

## Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

### Alternative: Single Staff Class with a Type Field

Instead of inheritance, all staff members can be represented using a single class containing a type field and a switch statement inside pay().

### Code

```cpp
class Staff
{
public:
    int type;
    double basic, research, hours, rate;

    double pay()
    {
        switch (type)
        {
            case 1:
                return basic + basic * 0.42 + research;

            case 2:
                return basic + basic * 0.42;

            case 3:
                return hours * rate;

            default:
                return 0;
        }
    }
};
```

### Comparison

| Feature | Inheritance | Single Class with Switch |
|---|---|---|
| Time per pay() call | O(1) | O(1) |
| Space per object | Stores only required fields | Stores all fields, including unused fields |
| Adding a new staff type | Add a new derived class | Modify the class and switch |
| Code maintenance | Easier | Becomes more complicated |
| Polymorphism | Supported | Not naturally supported |

### Conclusion

Inheritance is a better design because it avoids unnecessary data members, supports polymorphism, and allows new staff categories to be added without modifying existing classes.

